<div id="toggle-button">
  <section-header/>

  <h3 id="toggle-button-default">Minimal Layout</h3>

  <p>
    The default, minimal toggle button should be used when the contents are
    single-line and very short, as in shoe sizing, for example. This layout is
    geared more towards allowing the display of many more options and maximizing
    screen real estate.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>NOTE:</strong> All toggle button examples utilize JavaScript to
    toggle the <code>aria-pressed</code> state of the button.
  </p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="minimal-layout/toggledOff"
  />

  <h3 id="toggle-button-list">List Layout</h3>

  <p>
    List layout should be used when it's desired to have wider horizontal
    buttons. These are more easily stackable on narrower screens.
  </p>

  <p>
    You may use this layout by adding the
    <code>toggle-button--list-layout</code> modifier to the toggle button.
  </p>

  <h4>List Toggle Button with Title</h4>

  <component-demo component="toggle-button" variant="list-layout/titleOnly"/>

  <h4>List Toggle Button with Title and Subtitle</h4>

  <p>A subtitle can be added via <code>toggle-button__subtitle</code> block.</p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/titleSubtitle"
  />

  <h4>List Toggle Button with Title and Multi-line Subtitle</h4>

  <p>
    To have a multi-line subtitle, you may use a single <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>
    tag that has longer text or a maximum of two separate <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>
    tags with shorter text inside the <code>toggle-button__subtitle</code>
    block.
  </p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/titleMultilineSubtitle"
  />

  <h4>List Toggle Button with Icon, Title, and Subtitle</h4>

  <p>
    To add an icon to a toggle button, you can use the
    <code>toggle-button__icon</code> block.
  </p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/iconTitleSubtitle"
  />

  <h4>List Toggle Button with Image, Title, and Subtitle</h4>

  <p>
    To add an image to a toggle button, you can use the
    <code>toggle-button__image-container</code> block using an
    <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or as a <code>css</code> background image.
  </p>

  <h5>Using an Image Element</h5>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/profileImageTitleSubtitle"
  />

  <h5>Using a Contained CSS Background Image</h5>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/profileCSSImageContainBackground"
  />

  <h5>Using a Cover CSS Background Image</h5>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="list-layout/profileCSSImageCoverBackground"
  />

  <h3 id="toggle-button-gallery">Gallery Layout</h3>

  <p>
    Gallery layout should be used when there will be fewer buttons (as larger
    buttons take up more screen real estate) and it's desired to have a more
    vertical/square implementations with more emphasis on graphical elements
    (images/icons). As such, since the purpose of gallery layout buttons is to
    exentuate graphical elements, a graphical element (icon or image) is
    typically required in gallery layout buttons.
  </p>

  <p>
    You may use this layout by adding the
    <code>toggle-button--gallery-layout</code> modifier to the toggle button.
  </p>

  <h4>Gallery Toggle Button with Image</h4>

  <p>
    To add an image to a toggle button, you can use the
    <code>toggle-button__image-container</code> block.
  </p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout/profileImageTitleSubtitle"
  />

  <h3>Gallery Toggle Button Responsive Adjustments</h3>

  <p>
    Toggle buttons have a mininum and maximum width and are meant to be fluid
    between those boundaries. If using these buttons separately, you will need
    to set up their combined high-level layouts on your own. In these examples,
    their widths are artificially/manually set to various widths to demonstrate
    how the buttons undergo responsive adjustments.
  </p>

  <p>
    The image used in these buttons will shrink/grow responsively in width and
    height based on the overall button width.
  </p>

  <h4>Responsive Images Using Image Element</h4>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout-responsive/smallButtonWithImage"
  />
  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout-responsive/mediumButtonWithImage"
  />
  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout-responsive/largeButtonWithImage"
  />

  <h4>Responsive Images Using CSS Background-Image</h4>

  <p>
    These buttons use <code>background-size: contain;</code> for background
    image scaling/filling.
  </p>

  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout-responsive/smallButtonWithCSSImageContainBackground"
  />
  <component-demo
    component="toggle-button"
    variant="gallery-layout-responsive/mediumButtonWithCSSImageContainBackground"
